WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.069 --> 00:00:03.550
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.540
Pixar's seventeenth feature film Finding Dory
is stuffed to the gills with easter eggs.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:12.809
The usual suspects are all in there: A113,
the Pizza Planet Truck, the Luxo Ball, previous

00:00:12.809 --> 00:00:17.429
and upcoming Pixar movies, as well as Star
Wars, Disney, and other classic movie references.

00:00:17.429 --> 00:00:21.990
So, in this video, I'm going to look at all
the easter eggs, references, and cameos I

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:23.240
found in the movie.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.980
Ok, just before I wade in, I've got a really
special "The Art of Finding Dory" book to

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:30.640
give away from my friends at Abrams &amp; Chronicle.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.510
It's packed with beautiful concept art, sketches,
and information that give a glimpse into the

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:35.510
making of the movie.

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:39.920
For a chance to win, all you have to do is
subscribe and leave a comment on this video

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.230
about your favourite egg or reference in Finding
Dory or let me know about one I missed.

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:47.739
And of course, there are spoilers ahead, so
if you're not ready for that, check out my

00:00:47.739 --> 00:00:51.610
non-spoiler review of the movie right here!

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.690
There are some nice Pixar easter eggs hidden
in the quarantine lab.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.110
Stuck to the wall behind the fish tank is
a picture of Darla the fish-killing niece

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:02.020
of Dentist Sherman in Finding Nemo.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.890
The lonely volcano from the short film Lava,
which played before Inside Out, also appears

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.490
on a fridge magnet to the right of the scene.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:15.110
And there's an easter egg for Piper, the beautiful
Pixar short which played before Finding Dory.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:19.570
The cute sandpiper hatchling gets a visual
shout-out in a poster behind Hank as he scoops

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:23.329
Dory out of the fish tank with the coffee
jug.

00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:27.490
Pixar's movie WALL-E gets a well-hidden reference
in the scene where Hank is about to drop Dory

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.490
into the huge fish tank at the aquarium.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:34.270
There's a calendar with WALL-E written on
it in the background of that scene.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:38.770
The turtle Crush, of course, appears again
in Finding Dory helping Marlin, Nemo and Dory

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:42.680
get to Morro Bay, but a model of him also
hangs from the ceiling in the aquarium at

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.970
the Marine Life Institute.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.119
A113 appears twice in Finding Dory.

00:01:48.119 --> 00:01:52.370
The first time it flashes on and off the screen
very quickly on the back flippers of sea lions

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.920
Fluke and Rudder, which are tagged A1 and
13.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.789
By the way, those sea lions are voiced by
Idris Elba and Dominic West, who starred in

00:01:59.789 --> 00:02:01.190
The Wire together.

00:02:01.190 --> 00:02:06.079
The second time A113 appears is much easier
to spot as it's on the license plate of the

00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:09.270
truck transporting Hank, Marlin and Nemo to
Cleveland.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.420
The license plate reads CAL-A-1-1-3.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.890
The A113 easter egg has appeared in every
single Pixar movie to date and is a homage

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:21.270
to the classroom at California Institute of
the Arts where the likes of Pixar's John Lasseter

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.710
and Finding Dory director Andrew Stanton studied.

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:29.970
There's a really sneaky Luxo Ball easter egg
which you can spot very briefly in the centre

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:33.150
of the steering wheel as Hank is driving off
with the transport truck.

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:37.360
And interestingly, the way it appears also
looks rather like Captain America's shield

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.380
too.

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:43.600
The Luxo ball goes back Pixar's very first
animation, a short film called Luxo Jr, which

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:50.730
features both the Luxo ball and two desk lamps,
one of which appears in Pixar's logo.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:54.950
Director Andrew Stanton's confirmed that the
Pizza Planet truck appears early in the movie.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:58.281
And recently when I spoke with the movie's
Director of Photography, Ian Megibben, he

00:02:58.281 --> 00:03:02.120
told me it's around the sequence that features
what he called 'the movie's monster'.

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:05.590
And actually what that means is it's hidden
among the objects scattered in the shipping

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:08.980
lanes before Dory, Marlin and Nemo meet the
giant squid.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.620
And in the highway scene where Hank commandeers
the truck, there are also glimpses of various

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.970
cars on the road which look quite a bit like
the Pizza Planet truck.

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:22.401
Oh, and Ian Megibben also told me that there's
an easter egg to Pixar's next movie Cars 3

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:25.720
"close to where we see the Luxo Ball" in the
movie.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.500
So, look out for that!

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:32.890
There's another sneaky easter egg on one of
the pipes at the Marine Life Institute which

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:36.170
says "Seawater Supply TL59" on it.

00:03:36.170 --> 00:03:40.550
That phrase actually appears on a pipe above
the line for the Submarine Voyage attraction

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:46.560
at Disneyland, with TL standing for Tomorrowland
and 59 being the year the ride was opened.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:51.230
Interestingly enough, that attraction was
actually re-purposed in 2007 with a Finding

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:54.010
Nemo theme!

00:03:54.010 --> 00:03:59.480
The number 3181 on the tag Dory gets at the
Marine Life Institute is a triple Star Wars

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:01.130
and Pixar easter egg!

00:04:01.130 --> 00:04:07.560
The Stormtrooper FN-3181 appears in director
JJ Abrams' Star Wars: The Force Awakens.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.280
And that Stormtrooper just happens to be played
by regular Pixar composer Michael Giacchino,

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:16.470
who's also composed the music for many of
JJ Abram's other productions.

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:21.780
And on top of that, his Stormtrooper number
3181 can be rearranged as 1138, making it

00:04:21.780 --> 00:04:28.290
a hidden nod to George Lucas' 1971 film THX
1138.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:32.540
The touch pool scene at the aquarium has a
horror movie feel about with the fish cowering

00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:35.830
in fear when the children's hands start plunging
into the water.

00:04:35.830 --> 00:04:39.340
And it's actually reminiscent of two moments
in the Toy Story movies.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:42.900
There's the time the children run rampage
with Woody and his friends in the playroom

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:48.180
in Toy Story 3, and the children's hands smashing
in to the touch pool are also evocative of

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:54.230
the giant claw game Sid plays in the original
Toy Story.

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:57.900
Marlin's line that "crossing the ocean is
the kind of thing you should only do once"

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:02.190
is a meta-reference to the filmmakers' decision
to keep the journey from the coral reef to

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:06.750
California just a short scene as they didn't
want Finding Dory to be another road movie

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.060
like Finding Nemo.

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:12.480
And when Hank fills the touch pool with ink
and then apologises, it's a call back to how

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:16.630
the baby squid in Finding Nemo inks itself
when one of his friends pretends to push him

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:17.630
off the Drop-off.

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:22.500
There's a nod to the moment where Dory calls
the baby jellyfish "Squishy" in Finding Nemo

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:26.570
when she's searching for her parents in the
kelp forest and says she likes sand because

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.750
"sand is squishy."

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:35.530
Pixar itself gets an easter egg in the form
of the numbers 1200 and 86 both of which appear

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:38.100
on the side of the speedboat that picks up
Dory.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:43.280
The numbers are a reference to Pixar's address
at 1200 Park Avenue, Emeryville and to the

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:46.150
year that Pixar was founded, 1986.

00:05:46.150 --> 00:05:51.480
Disney's VW Beetle Herbie which has featured
in a number of movies over the years looks

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:56.400
like it may have sadly ended up rusting on
the bottom of the ocean floor in Finding Dory.

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.200
A funny moment in the film occurs when Dory
swims to the surface near the Marine Life

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.950
Institute and says "Hello" and she hears a
voice respond "Hello, I'm Sigourney Weaver".

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:09.280
Dory then has an hilarious discussion with
the (automated) voice of Sigourney Weaver

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.580
playing over the tannoy at the Institute.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.870
This is a clever nod to Weaver's role as the
voice of the ship's computer in Pixar's WALL-E.

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:20.200
And there's a really neat reference to one
of Weaver's most iconic movies, Alien, in

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:24.670
the scene where Bailey sees what he thinks
is Dory getting eaten alive by another huge

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:26.060
fish in the pipes.

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:30.080
The way Bailey sees this happen using his
echolocation is an homage to the scene from

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.590
Alien where the character Dallas get taken
by the alien while stuck in a ventilation

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:35.590
shaft.

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:40.010
The pipes sequence may also be one of the
two references the filmmakers have said they

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.700
made to Die Hard in Finding Dory after they
saw a spoof of how all the Pixar movies are

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:46.670
connected to the Die Hard movie franchise.

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:51.120
And the second Die Hard reference comes up
when Dory is looking for Hank in the touch

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:52.120
pool.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.740
The other fish shout out "Hands, Hands" to
warn her the kids are coming, but Dory misunderstands

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:00.050
and replies, "No, not Hans, I'm looking for
Hank."

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.970
Hans Gruber is, of course, the villain from
the first Die Hard movie.

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:08.980
After the movie's credits have rolled, the
Tank Gang from Finding Nemo makes their return!

00:07:08.980 --> 00:07:13.960
It's a funny moment when Nemo's old friends
roll up in front of the rock where sea lions

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:15.650
Rudder and Fluke are sleeping.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:19.419
The gang are all still in the plastic bags
they were in last time we saw them, but everyone's

00:07:19.419 --> 00:07:22.620
bag has turned green with algae except for
Jacques.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.370
It's a fun call back to the first movie when
the Tank Gang deliberately made their fish

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:31.650
tank go green by sabotaging their water filter
and had to stop Jacques' natural inclination

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:32.650
to clean the tank.

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:33.650
Sadly, Jacques' original voice actor Joe Ranft
died long before Finding Dory was made, but

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:34.650
his brother Jerome, who also works at Pixar,
stepped in to the role for this movie.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:39.210
There are a bunch of cool cameos in Finding
Dory including Pixar's lucky charm John Ratzenberger

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:43.270
who plays the husband crab Bill who you see
cutting grass while Dory asks his wife for

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:45.290
directions to the quarantine lab.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:49.840
Alexander Gould, who voiced Nemo in the original
film 13 years ago, cameos as the voice of

00:07:49.840 --> 00:07:51.520
the truck driver.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:55.610
Bill Hader, who was the voice of Fear in Inside
Out, plays the voice of Stan the husband fish

00:07:55.610 --> 00:07:59.430
who appears at the beginning of the movie
when baby Dory is lost and looking for her

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:00.430
family.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:04.020
The fact Hader's character and Dory are talking
about short-term memory loss seems nicely

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:09.240
appropriate not only for Dory's situation
here but also because of Hader's role in Inside

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:11.590
Out, which was all about memory.

00:08:11.590 --> 00:08:16.080
By the way, Hader's wife fish is voiced by
his SNL co-star Kate McKinnon.

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.080
Director Andrew Stanton is back voicing Crush
the sea turtle again, but he also lends his

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:24.040
voice to the clam that Marlin and Nemo encounter
after they escape from the fish tank at the

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:25.040
gift shop.

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.040
And there's an air of Little Shop of Horrors'
bloodthirsty flytrap-like plant in the look

00:08:30.040 --> 00:08:31.770
of the rather talkative clam.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:37.580
Other Pixar crew cameoing include film editor
Torbin Xan Bullock as the sea lion Gerald.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:42.039
Curiously Gerald is the same name as the pelican
who chokes on Marlin and Dory in Finding Nemo,

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.730
and both Geralds have a similar comedic function
as the butt of jokes.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:50.390
And co-director Angus Maclane is the sunfish
"Charlie Back and Forth" who was also in Finding

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.390
Nemo.

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:54.670
So, were there any cool easter eggs or references
you spotted that I missed?

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:57.270
And who was your favourite new character in
Finding Dory?

